# os/macos.capabilities — the macOS capability declaration (Core v5, #663/#667).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read (never sourced) by Core's zsh/02-capabilities.zsh into $_CORE_CAP, and by
# maint/dotfiles-maint.sh, which is bash. bootstrap.sh links it to
# $ZDOTDIR/os.capabilities via blib_link_os_layer.
#
# THE SOURCE of core/PORTING-MATRIX.md §"Package-manager commands", macOS column —
# the table is rendered from this file (dotfiles-core#686): edit here, then run
# `make gen-porting-matrix` in dotfiles-core.
# Validate with:  core/scripts/check-capabilities.sh os/macos.capabilities
#
# NOTHING HERE IS PRIVILEGED, and that is the one rule this file exists to state.
# Every other repo in the fleet prefixes its verbs with `sudo`; Homebrew refuses to
# run as root and a `sudo brew` is how you break a prefix's permissions for good.
# Core's _pkgup_run passes an unprefixed value straight through, so the absence of
# the prefix IS the declaration.
#
# A `#` INSIDE A VALUE IS NOT A COMMENT — the reader keeps it. Notes go on their own
# line, above.

# ── package-manager verbs (all required) ──────────────────────────────────────
PKG_REFRESH=brew update
PKG_UPGRADE=brew upgrade
PKG_INSTALL=brew install
PKG_REMOVE=brew uninstall
PKG_SEARCH=brew search
# `brew which-formula` needs the homebrew/command-not-found tap; without it this
# verb reports the tap is missing rather than answering, which is the honest failure.
PKG_OWNS=brew which-formula
PKG_COUNT_PENDING=brew outdated --quiet
# --quiet prints one bare formula name per line, so the default match (`.`) and
# field (1) are exactly right and no PKG_PENDING_* key is needed.

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SCHEDULER=launchd
# An OS-ABSOLUTE PATH IS CORRECT HERE and wrong in Core — this key is what got the
# last one out of zsh/55-maint.zsh, where `~/Library/LaunchAgents` appeared at six
# sites and was true on exactly one of the fleet's nine boxes. A DIRECTORY: Core
# appends its own unit name (com.dotfiles.maint.plist).
SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR=~/Library/LaunchAgents

# ── how `up` applies ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Homebrew computes against a stale index without this, so the refresh is a real
# precondition rather than a courtesy: a failure here ABORTS the upgrade.
PKG_UPGRADE_PRE=brew update
PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL=brew upgrade
PKG_CLEANUP=brew cleanup
# NO PKG_ASSUME_YES: `brew upgrade` does not prompt, so there is no flag to append
# and inventing one would be a lie. `up -y` behaves like `up`, correctly.

# ── how `up` counts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE ONE ARCHIVE THAT NEEDS THIS. `brew outdated` reads a cached index, so without a
# preceding `brew update` the nudge reports a number that can never change. Declared
# on the COUNT path only — the list path deliberately does not pay for the network.
PKG_COUNT_REFRESH=brew update

# ── the scheduled runner ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and this is NOT the rolling-distro
# reason Arch and Gentoo omit it. maint/dotfiles-maint.sh ALREADY runs brew
# update/upgrade/cleanup unconditionally, before the system-apply block it reaches —
# which is why that block's brew arm is a bare `:` with the comment "brew already
# upgraded above". Declaring the flag would make the declared path run `brew upgrade`
# a SECOND time in the same run. Omitting it reproduces today's behaviour exactly.

# ── optional tools ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO TOOLS_OPTIN, deliberately. A declared list REPLACES Core's _CORE_DOCTOR_OPTIN
# rather than adding to it, so declaring one means re-stating every optional tool.
# PORTING-MATRIX.md's ²¹ table has no macOS column to derive a delta from — the
# Brewfile installs the stack — so Core's default is the only honest answer and is
# also what this box gets today.
